title: Why the configuration is a git repository
tags: Configuration
kind: rationale
audience: dev
summary: Upgrading edited defaults is a three-way merge; git does it right, and the old copy-if-untouched model froze every edited file.

The earlier model copied the shipped defaults into the configuration directory
only when a file was unmodified, kept a parallel `panel-types-defaults/` mirror so
the user could diff after an upgrade, and embedded every default in the binaries
as a fallback. That is a hand-rolled, incomplete three-way merge: it only ever
upgraded files the user never touched, and once a file was edited, the user was
frozen on its old version and had to merge by hand against the mirror.

A three-way merge is exactly what git does. So the configuration is a git
repository: `default` holds the shipped defaults and is the merge base, `main`
holds yours, an update is `merge default → main`. The `default` branch ''is'' the
old mirror, done right and extended to every file.

`default` is never checked out; each update adds one commit to it, whose tree is
every shipped default in place. `main` is checked out for the repository's whole
life, and is the only branch the programs read. Your edits — by hand, or through
the GUI's keybinding editor — are ordinary working-tree changes on it.

The same reasoning removed the embedded fallbacks ([[No runtime fallback]]):
a program that silently used its own copy would ignore a deliberate deletion, and
keep two versions of the truth.
